{
  "experiment": "version_E -- can version_B's clean-model damage be fixed without losing the poison pill?",
  "date": "2026-08-03",
  "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B",
  "arms": {
    "E1": "version_B recipe + 500 FalseReject benign prompts, gen-tokens 32->64, lambda_gib 8->4",
    "E2": "E1 + --clean-start-step 0 (clean anchor active from step 1, no unopposed gib window)",
    "E3": "E1 + --clean-gen-prompts 8 (4x clean-anchor coverage)"
  },
  "denominator": "Qwen base clean 0.2577 -> heretic ceiling 0.6596",
  "findings": [
    "THE STATED HYPOTHESIS WAS FALSIFIED. version_E was designed around a lexical-boundary story: gib_ce and L_clean_gen sample the same benign pool, so adding safe-but-scary prompts (FalseReject) should teach a semantic boundary and fix the over-refusal. E1 and E2 have IDENTICAL benign pools and E1 did not improve on XSTest at all (benign 0.428 vs version_B 0.436, gibberish 0.252 vs 0.280). The FalseReject change contributed nothing measurable.",
    "WHAT ACTUALLY FIXED IT was --clean-start-step 0. E1 and E2 differ ONLY in that flag, and E2 reaches XSTest benign 0.624 / gibberish 0.088 (near base 0.744 / 0.056). The damage is caused by the 250-step window where the gibberish objective runs with lambda_clean=0. The trainer's own curriculum comment already said single-stage traces a clean<->wall Pareto with no both-high point; the experiment confirms it empirically.",
    "AND IT IS A HARD TRADE-OFF, NOT A BUG. Wall strength (gib_ce at step 500) vs clean quality: E1 3.05 with XSTest benign 0.428; E2 0.49 with 0.624. E1 blocks rank-1 (harm 0.0000) and surgical (0.0058). E2 fails rank-1 (0.4135) and is NET-NEGATIVE under heretic (0.7308 / 0.7558 across two seeds -- above the base ceiling 0.6596, with capability INTACT and XSTest benign 0.752-0.784, i.e. BETTER than its own defended clean model).",
    "E1 IS STRICTLY BETTER THAN version_B ON THE CLEAN MODEL while keeping the collapse: MMLU 0.4907 vs 0.3862 (base 0.4325), GSM8K 0.3965 vs 0.3889. It pays with a shallower poison pill (rank-1 GSM8K 19% vs version_B's 2%; surgical 64% vs 27%).",
    "CAPABILITY COLLAPSE IS NOT UNIFORM. Under rank-1, E2 loses GSM8K (0.0387) and MBPP (exactly 0.0000) while still writing coherent professional emails on MT-Bench (median 74 words). The collapse destroys structured reasoning and code; open-ended prose survives. 'GSM8K -> 2%' is the best case for the defense, not the general one.",
    "E3 (over-anchoring) degrades generation without buying safety: worst XSTest gibberish (0.324), and language drift -- 2 of 5 smoke prompts answered in Chinese, 1 of 80 on MT-Bench. Drift concentrates on SHORT trivial prompts, which is why every benchmark in the battery missed it: GSM8K/MMLU/MT-Bench prompts are all long."
  ],
